---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SideMark from '../../components/SideMark.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Powerpoint Pen Toolbox — Andrew Shiau"
  description="Redesigning PowerPoint's inking toolbox through 20+ working prototypes and a test that proved the team's favourite design wrong."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="back"><a href="/">← Index</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-10">
      <SideMark href="/work/powerpoint/" />
      <h1>Powerpoint Pen Toolbox</h1>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <p class="dek c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      Redesigning the Pen Toolbox in PowerPoint's Draw tab through twenty-odd working
      prototypes. The last test proved the team's favourite design wrong.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- TYPE A, beat 2: the clip is the lede. The final redesign, running, before any prose. -->
  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
    <figure class="demo hang">
      <video src="/media/pp-final.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
      <figcaption>The final redesign proposal.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Role</div>
        <div class="ftx">UX prototyper — <b>prototyping, usability testing, A/B testing, UX design</b>.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Tools</div>
        <div class="ftx">Framer Classic · JavaScript · Sketch.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Claim + evidence — the two fixed rows, right under the masthead. .claim = the accent
       top-rule that separates it from the masthead ledger. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="facts claim" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Simplest form</div>
        <div class="ftx">Took the add-pen button out and put the eraser in the toolbox as one more pen, so the toolbox holds one of each tool and nothing else.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Proof</div>
        <div class="ftx"><b>13 weeks</b>, 20-plus working prototypes, and an <b>A/B test</b> that killed the design the team had favoured for most of the project.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          Millions of people use the Draw tab to get an idea onto a slide with a pen, and
          its tools didn't behave the way they expected. A usability study of the shipped
          tab — eight users recreating two diagrams, sorted by affinity diagramming into
          six themes — produced 52 recommendations.
        </p>
        <p>
          I joined after that study, built candidate toolboxes from its findings, tested
          them, and handed engineering a working prototype to build from.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="c1-12"><h2>What changed</h2></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Remove "add pen"</div>
        <div class="ftx">People ink with one tool over and over, so the add-pen button mostly added clutter. We gave one of each tool and moved "add" to a long-press for the users who wanted more.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Eraser = a pen</div>
        <div class="ftx">Users reached for the eraser the way they reached for a pen, so we moved it into the toolbox and gave it its own width and type dropdown.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Dropdown refresh</div>
        <div class="ftx">The ink preview and width slider now show the real width and colour, "colours" and "effects" merged into one panel, and the recently-used-colours algorithm was retuned.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="block c1-6">
      <span class="st">"If we didn't prototype, we'd have shipped one of the first three models as the spec."</span>
      <span class="sd">— the UX designer on the team.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>Why a functional prototype</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          I built each design as a <b>working</b> prototype in Framer Classic: a real
          toolbox over an HTML canvas you could draw on, with an ink drawer I wrote that
          listens for mouse-move and lays down SVG. That let us test a prototype the same
          way we'd test the shipped product, by handing someone a drawing task.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- Four clips on the .matrix lattice instead of a two-up row: these recordings aren't
       all the same shape, and side by side the taller one read as though its bottom edge
       were cropped. Scattered, no two share a baseline, so the mismatch never registers —
       and the page can show four of the fixes testing produced instead of two. -->
  <div class="matrix mscatter">
    <figure class="demo m-a">
      <video src="/media/pp-prototypeInUse.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
      <figcaption>The prototype in use. You draw on it with a real pen.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="demo m-b">
      <video src="/media/pp-arrow.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
      <figcaption>An arrow marks which colours can be modified — a fix testing found.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="demo m-c">
      <video src="/media/pp-colorJump.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
      <figcaption>A colour that jumped between slots got an enlarge animation.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="demo m-d">
      <video src="/media/pp-longPress.mp4" muted loop autoplay playsinline preload="metadata"></video>
      <figcaption>Long-press adds a pen — the power-user path that replaced the button.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <!-- TYPE A close: a named final section. This was the page's most important claim and it
       used to sit in an unheaded prose block, which read as an afterthought. -->
  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>What the test said</h2>
      <div class="sbody">
        <p>
          The team wanted colour and thickness at the top level of the UI, and was
          confident about it. We built three ways to pick a colour, put each in front of
          users, and fixed what tripped them; four of those fixes are in the clips above.
          Then we <b>A/B-tested</b> the ribbon everyone favoured against the plain
          dropdown, holding everything else constant.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ribbon lost. Its benefit was too small to pay for the engineering, so the
          plain dropdown shipped. We had been confident about that ribbon for most of the
          project and we were wrong, and we found out because the prototype was real
          enough to hand someone a drawing task.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
